Koda runs in two modes:

  INTERACTIVE   Run `koda` (no arguments) to open the full TUI.
                Type your question and press Enter.
                Type /help inside for keybindings and all commands.

  EXEC          `koda exec` runs one prompt without the TUI and exits.
                For scripts, pipes and CI pipelines.
                  koda exec "explain this codebase"
                  git diff | koda exec
                  koda exec --json "fix the failing tests"

Configuration precedence (highest wins):
  1. CLI flags     --model, --provider, --base-url, --mode, ...
  2. -c key=value  a config.toml key, for this run only
  3. Config file   ~/.config/koda/config.toml, which /model and
                   /provider write and you can edit
  4. Built-in defaults

API keys (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY, …)
follow the same order. Keys saved with /key are loaded from the
local keystore at startup and injected as env vars — shell env
vars always win over stored keys.

Usage: koda [OPTIONS] [PROMPT] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  exec    Run one prompt without the TUI, then exit
  resume  Resume a saved session in the TUI, as `codex resume` does: by its id (or the start of one), the most recent with --last, or pick one from a list
  fork    Fork a saved session and open the copy in the TUI, as `codex fork` does: by its id (or the start of one), the most recent with --last, or pick one from a list. The original stays as it was. With --worktree (and an id), the copy opens in a new worktree
  serve   Serve the client protocol on stdin and stdout, for a GUI, an editor or a script
  mcp     Manage the MCP servers every session starts: list, get, add, remove
  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [PROMPT]
          Open the TUI and send this prompt, as `codex "prompt"` does. A one-shot run is `koda exec "prompt"`

Options:
  -s, --resume <SESSION>
          Session ID to resume (omit to start a new session)

      --base-url <BASE_URL>
          LLM provider base URL override

      --model <MODEL>
          Model name override

      --provider <PROVIDER>
          LLM provider (openai, anthropic, lmstudio, llama-cpp, gemini, groq, grok, ollama)

      --oss
          Run on a local open-weight model, as Codex's `--oss` does. Koda starts llama.cpp's llama-server with the model (Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on a 128 GB Mac, else LFM3-1.7B, a 1 GB quick try), and stops it when Koda exits. `oss_model` in config.toml picks another

      --local-provider <PROVIDER>
          The local provider for --oss: llama-cpp (the default, started and stopped by Koda), lm-studio or ollama (connected to)

      --worktree
          Run the session in a new managed Git worktree, as Codex's `--worktree` does: a detached checkout of the project's HEAD under Koda's config folder (worktrees/), outside the repository. Kept when Koda exits; remove it with `git worktree remove <path>`. With `koda fork <id>`, the fork opens in a checkout of the repository the session worked in

  -a, --agent <AGENT>
          Agent to use: a built-in, or a JSON file in .koda/agents/ or ~/.config/koda/agents/ (default: config.toml's `agent`, else `default`)

      --project-root <PROJECT_ROOT>
          Project root directory (defaults to current directory)

      --max-context-tokens <TOKENS>
          Context window in tokens, for servers that don't report theirs. Beats the agent config, the server's answer and the model table

      --reasoning-effort <LEVEL>
          Reasoning effort. Claude: low, medium, high, xhigh or max; by default the level Claude Code uses for the model. OpenAI-compatible servers: passed through as `reasoning_effort`
          
          [alias: --effort]

      --mode <MODE>
          Trust mode: auto (the default, unless config.toml sets `mode`), safe, or plan. "auto" auto-approves mutating actions; the kernel sandbox contains them. Destructive ops (rm -rf, git reset --hard, git push --force) and outside-project writes still confirm. Auto requires the kernel sandbox; on unsandboxed platforms koda refuses to start with an actionable error (use --mode safe or install the platform sandbox backend). "safe" confirms every mutation before executing, for when you want a person to approve each change. "plan" only looks: reads, searches, read-only commands and MCP tools marked read-only run; everything else is refused, and the kernel sandbox keeps the project read-only. In the TUI, Koda then proposes a plan and asks whether to carry it out. Sandbox with credential protection is always active
          
          [possible values: safe, auto, plan]

      --mcp-config <FILE|JSON>
          Add MCP servers for this run only, from a JSON file or inline JSON in the `{"mcpServers": {...}}` shape of Claude Code's `.mcp.json`. Repeatable; later servers win a name clash, and all of them win over servers stored with `koda mcp add`. Nothing is saved

  -i, --image <FILE>
          Images to attach to the first prompt, as Codex's `-i` does: a path, or several separated by commas. Repeatable

  -c, --config <KEY=VALUE>
          Override a config.toml value for this run: `-c key=value`, with a dotted key (`-c model_providers.lm-studio.base_url=http://...`). The value is read as TOML, else taken as text, as Codex's `-c` is, and replaces what is at that key. Repeatable; never saved. Flags such as --reasoning-effort are `-c` values too, and win over these

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Examples:
  koda                                     # interactive TUI (type /help inside)
  koda "fix the failing test"              # the TUI, starting with that prompt
  koda -s abc123                           # resume a saved session
  koda exec "explain this codebase"       # one-shot question, then exit
  koda exec - < prompt.txt                 # read the prompt from stdin
  git diff | koda exec                     # pipe a diff as the prompt
  koda exec --json "fix the failing tests" # the client protocol on stdout
  koda exec --model o3 "refactor"         # one-shot with a specific model
  koda exec -s abc123 "continue"          # continue a saved session
